Lab: Monitoring Cluster Health and Metrics with Prometheus and Grafana
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Deploy the Prometheus Operator and a Prometheus instance to monitor an OpenShift/Kubernetes cluster
Configure ServiceMonitors so Prometheus discovers and scrapes metrics targets
Deploy Grafana and connect it to Prometheus as a data source
Create Prometheus alerting rules for resource utilization thresholds and verify they load correctly
Apply basic troubleshooting techniques for common monitoring stack issues
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Kubernetes and OpenShift concepts (Deployments, Services, Namespaces)
Familiarity with YAML configuration files
Comfort with Linux command-line operations
Access to an OpenShift cluster with cluster-admin privileges and the oc CLI installed
This lab uses only the oc CLI and manifests applied directly to the cluster, so no additional local packages need to be installed. If you are working in Al Nafi's pre-configured cloud machine, oc is already installed and connected to a working cluster â click Start Lab to begin.

Verify your CLI and cluster connectivity before proceeding:

oc version --client
oc whoami
You should see the client version printed and your logged-in username returned. If oc whoami fails, log in first with oc login <cluster-api-url> using credentials provided by your lab environment.

Task 1: Deploy Prometheus and Configure Metric Scraping
Step 1.1: Create a Monitoring Namespace
oc new-project monitoring-lab
oc project monitoring-lab
Verify the namespace exists:

oc get project monitoring-lab
Deliverable: The command returns the monitoring-lab project with status Active.

Step 1.2: Deploy the Prometheus Operator
The Prometheus Operator manages Prometheus and Alertmanager instances declaratively through Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs). Most OpenShift clusters already ship the operator and CRDs as part of the built-in cluster-monitoring stack; this lab installs a second, independent instance in monitoring-lab so you can safely practice without touching cluster-wide monitoring.

Create the RBAC resources the operator needs:

cat << 'EOF' > prometheus-operator-rbac.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: prometheus-operator
  namespace: monitoring-lab
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: prometheus-operator-monitoring-lab
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["nodes", "services", "endpoints", "pods", "configmaps", "secrets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["statefulsets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "create", "update", "delete"]
- apiGroups: ["monitoring.coreos.com"]
  resources: ["*"]
  verbs: ["*"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: prometheus-operator-monitoring-lab
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: prometheus-operator-monitoring-lab
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: prometheus-operator
  namespace: monitoring-lab
EOF
oc apply -f prometheus-operator-rbac.yaml
Deploy the operator:

cat << 'EOF' > prometheus-operator.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: prometheus-operator
  namespace: monitoring-lab
  labels:
    app: prometheus-operator
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: prometheus-operator
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: prometheus-operator
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: prometheus-operator
      containers:
      - name: prometheus-operator
        image: quay.io/prometheus-operator/prometheus-operator:v0.71.2
        args:
        - --prometheus-config-reloader=quay.io/prometheus-operator/prometheus-config-reloader:v0.71.2
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080
          name: http
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: 100m
            memory: 100Mi
          limits:
            cpu: 200m
            memory: 200Mi
EOF
oc apply -f prometheus-operator.yaml
Verify the operator pod is running:

oc get pods -l app=prometheus-operator
oc wait --for=condition=Available deployment/prometheus-operator -n monitoring-lab --timeout=120s
Deliverable: oc get pods -l app=prometheus-operator shows a pod in Running status with 1/1 ready containers, and the oc wait command reports deployment.apps/prometheus-operator condition met.

Step 1.3: Deploy a Prometheus Instance
Create the ServiceAccount and RBAC Prometheus itself will run as:

cat << 'EOF' > prometheus-sa.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: prometheus
  namespace: monitoring-lab
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: prometheus-monitoring-lab
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["nodes", "services", "endpoints", "pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["configmaps"]
  verbs: ["get"]
- nonResourceURLs: ["/metrics"]
  verbs: ["get"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: prometheus-monitoring-lab
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: prometheus-monitoring-lab
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: prometheus
  namespace: monitoring-lab
EOF
oc apply -f prometheus-sa.yaml
Create the Prometheus custom resource:

cat << 'EOF' > prometheus-instance.yaml
apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: Prometheus
metadata:
  name: prometheus-main
  namespace: monitoring-lab
spec:
  serviceAccountName: prometheus
  serviceMonitorSelector:
    matchLabels:
      team: monitoring-lab
  ruleSelector:
    matchLabels:
      team: monitoring-lab
  resources:
    requests:
      memory: 400Mi
      cpu: 100m
    limits:
      memory: 800Mi
      cpu: 200m
  retention: 1d
  replicas: 1
EOF
oc apply -f prometheus-instance.yaml
Expose Prometheus with a Service and a Route:

cat << 'EOF' > prometheus-service.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: prometheus-service
  namespace: monitoring-lab
spec:
  selector:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: prometheus
    prometheus: prometheus-main
  ports:
  - name: web
    port: 9090
    targetPort: 9090
EOF
oc apply -f prometheus-service.yaml
oc expose service prometheus-service --name=prometheus-route
Verify Prometheus is running and reachable:

oc get prometheus
oc get pods -l app.kubernetes.io/name=prometheus
oc get route prometheus-route
Deliverable: oc get pods -l app.kubernetes.io/name=prometheus shows a pod named prometheus-prometheus-main-0 in Running state, and oc get route prometheus-route returns a hostname you can use to reach the Prometheus web UI.

Step 1.4: Deploy Node Exporter and Create a ServiceMonitor
Node Exporter exposes host-level metrics (CPU, memory, disk) that Prometheus can scrape.

cat << 'EOF' > node-exporter.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: node-exporter
  namespace: monitoring-lab
  labels:
    app: node-exporter
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: node-exporter
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: node-exporter
    spec:
      hostNetwork: true
      hostPID: true
      containers:
      - name: node-exporter
        image: quay.io/prometheus/node-exporter:v1.7.0
        args:
        - --path.procfs=/host/proc
        - --path.sysfs=/host/sys
        ports:
        - containerPort: 9100
          hostPort: 9100
          name: metrics
        volumeMounts:
        - name: proc
          mountPath: /host/proc
          readOnly: true
        - name: sys
          mountPath: /host/sys
          readOnly: true
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: 50m
            memory: 50Mi
          limits:
            cpu: 100m
            memory: 100Mi
      volumes:
      - name: proc
        hostPath:
          path: /proc
      - name: sys
        hostPath:
          path: /sys
      tolerations:
      - operator: Exists
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: node-exporter
  namespace: monitoring-lab
  labels:
    app: node-exporter
    team: monitoring-lab
spec:
  clusterIP: None
  ports:
  - name: metrics
    port: 9100
    targetPort: 9100
  selector:
    app: node-exporter
EOF
oc apply -f node-exporter.yaml
Create the ServiceMonitor that tells Prometheus to scrape Node Exporter. Note that the spec.selector.matchLabels here must match the labels on the node-exporter Service above, and the team: monitoring-lab label on the ServiceMonitor's own metadata must match Prometheus's serviceMonitorSelector from Step 1.3:

cat << 'EOF' > node-exporter-servicemonitor.yaml
apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: ServiceMonitor
metadata:
  name: node-exporter
  namespace: monitoring-lab
  labels:
    team: monitoring-lab
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: node-exporter
  endpoints:
  - port: metrics
    interval: 30s
    path: /metrics
EOF
oc apply -f node-exporter-servicemonitor.yaml
Verify Node Exporter pods are running and Prometheus has picked up the target:

oc get pods -l app=node-exporter
oc get servicemonitors
Deliverable: oc get pods -l app=node-exporter shows one Running pod per cluster node. oc get servicemonitors lists node-exporter. In the Prometheus web UI (reachable at the prometheus-route hostname), navigating to Status > Targets shows a node-exporter job with state UP.

Task 2: Deploy Grafana and Visualize Metrics
Step 2.1: Configure and Deploy Grafana
Create the datasource ConfigMap that points Grafana at the in-cluster Prometheus service:

cat << 'EOF' > grafana-datasources.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: grafana-datasources
  namespace: monitoring-lab
data:
  prometheus.yaml: |
    apiVersion: 1
    datasources:
    - name: Prometheus
      type: prometheus
      access: proxy
      url: http://prometheus-service:9090
      isDefault: true
      editable: true
EOF
oc apply -f grafana-datasources.yaml
Deploy Grafana:

cat << 'EOF' > grafana-deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: grafana
  namespace: monitoring-lab
  labels:
    app: grafana
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: grafana
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: grafana
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: grafana
        image: docker.io/grafana/grafana:10.4.2
        ports:
        - containerPort: 3000
        env:
        - name: GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD
          value: "admin123"
        volumeMounts:
        - name: grafana-storage
          mountPath: /var/lib/grafana
        - name: grafana-datasources
          mountPath: /etc/grafana/provisioning/datasources
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: 100m
            memory: 200Mi
          limits:
            cpu: 200m
            memory: 400Mi
      volumes:
      - name: grafana-storage
        emptyDir: {}
      - name: grafana-datasources
        configMap:
          name: grafana-datasources
EOF
oc apply -f grafana-deployment.yaml
Expose Grafana:

cat << 'EOF' > grafana-service.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: grafana-service
  namespace: monitoring-lab
spec:
  selector:
    app: grafana
  ports:
  - port: 3000
    targetPort: 3000
EOF
oc apply -f grafana-service.yaml
oc expose service grafana-service --name=grafana-route
Verify the deployment:

oc get pods -l app=grafana
oc get route grafana-route
Deliverable: oc get pods -l app=grafana shows a Running pod. Opening http://<grafana-route-host> in a browser and logging in with username admin and password admin123 shows the Grafana home page.

Step 2.2: Confirm the Prometheus Data Source and View Metrics
In the Grafana UI, go to Connections > Data sources and confirm Prometheus is listed and marked as the default source (provisioned automatically from the ConfigMap).
Click Explore, select the Prometheus data source, and run the query:
up
Run a second query to confirm Node Exporter metrics are flowing:
node_cpu_seconds_total
Deliverable: The up query returns a table/graph with one row per scrape target (including node-exporter) with a value of 1, confirming Prometheus is successfully collecting metrics that Grafana can visualize.

Task 3: Create and Verify Prometheus Alert Rules
Step 3.1: Create Alert Rules for Resource Utilization
Create a PrometheusRule resource with alerts for high CPU and high memory usage. The team: monitoring-lab label must match the ruleSelector configured on the Prometheus instance in Task 1:

cat << 'EOF' > cluster-alerts.yaml
apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: PrometheusRule
metadata:
  name: cluster-alerts
  namespace: monitoring-lab
  labels:
    team: monitoring-lab
spec:
  groups:
  - name: cluster.rules
    rules:
    - alert: HighNodeCPUUsage
      expr: 100 - (avg by (instance) (rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{mode="idle"}[5m])) * 100) > 80
      for: 5m
      labels:
        severity: warning
      annotations:
        summary: "High CPU usage on {{ $labels.instance }}"
        description: "CPU usage has been above 80 percent for more than 5 minutes."
    - alert: HighNodeMemoryUsage
      expr: (1 - (node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes / node_memory_MemTotal_bytes)) * 100 > 85
      for: 5m
      labels:
        severity: warning
      annotations:
        summary: "High memory usage on {{ $labels.instance }}"
        description: "Memory usage has been above 85 percent for more than 5 minutes."
EOF
oc apply -f cluster-alerts.yaml
Verify the rule object was created:

oc get prometheusrules
Deliverable: oc get prometheusrules lists cluster-alerts.

Step 3.2: Verify Prometheus Loaded the Rules
Query the Prometheus API directly to confirm the rules were picked up (this avoids relying on browser access and works from the command line):

PROMETHEUS_HOST=$(oc get route prometheus-route -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
curl -s "http://${PROMETHEUS_HOST}/api/v1/rules" | grep -o '"name":"[A-Za-z]*"'
Deliverable: The output includes "name":"HighNodeCPUUsage" and "name":"HighNodeMemoryUsage", confirming Prometheus loaded the PrometheusRule resource through the ruleSelector match. You can also check target and rule health visually at Status > Rules in the Prometheus web UI.

Expected Outcomes
A running Prometheus instance in the monitoring-lab namespace scraping metrics from Node Exporter via a correctly labeled ServiceMonitor
A Grafana deployment provisioned with a working Prometheus data source, capable of querying live cluster metrics
Two active Prometheus alerting rules (HighNodeCPUUsage, HighNodeMemoryUsage) confirmed loaded through the Prometheus HTTP API
Troubleshooting
Issue 1: ServiceMonitor Target Shows as "Down" or Missing in Prometheus
Symptom: The node-exporter job does not appear, or appears with state DOWN, under Status > Targets in the Prometheus UI.

Diagnostic hint: Compare the labels on the ServiceMonitor's spec.selector.matchLabels against the actual labels on the target Service, and compare the ServiceMonitor's own metadata labels against the Prometheus serviceMonitorSelector. A mismatch in either place is the most common cause. Check both directly:

oc get service node-exporter -o jsonpath='{.metadata.labels}'
oc get servicemonitor node-exporter -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector.matchLabels}'
oc get servicemonitor node-exporter -o jsonpath='{.metadata.labels}'
oc get prometheus prometheus-main -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceMonitorSelector.matchLabels}'
If the label sets shown by the first two commands do not intersect, or the last two do not match, update the mismatched manifest and reapply it with oc apply -f.

Issue 2: Grafana Dashboard Shows "No Data"
Symptom: Explore or dashboard panels in Grafana return empty results even though Prometheus targets are UP.

Diagnostic hint: Confirm Grafana can actually reach the Prometheus service URL configured in the datasource, and confirm the metric name used in the query exists in Prometheus at all:

oc exec deployment/grafana -n monitoring-lab -- curl -s http://prometheus-service:9090/api/v1/query?query=up
If this returns a valid JSON result but the Grafana panel is still empty, check the panel's time range and PromQL syntax rather than the network path.

Conclusion
In this lab you deployed a self-contained Prometheus and Grafana monitoring stack in its own namespace, using the Prometheus Operator to manage a Prometheus instance, a Node Exporter DaemonSet for host metrics, and a ServiceMonitor to wire the two together. You connected Grafana to Prometheus as a provisioned data source and confirmed metrics were queryable end to end, then defined and verified Prometheus alerting rules for high CPU and memory usage directly through the Prometheus HTTP API. Along the way you practiced the label-matching relationships that tie ServiceMonitors, Services, and PrometheusRules to a Prometheus instance's selectors, which is the most common source of "missing metrics" or "missing alerts" problems in real deployments. For production use, extend this pattern by adding Alertmanager with real notification receivers, persistent storage for Prometheus and Grafana instead of emptyDir, and stricter RBAC scoped to only the resources each component needs.
